{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791138027,"nonce":"+AdnjJTvxv1dQi0mBgU52L1rgds=","title":"Inline markup: field completion inside {expr} gives the top-level list","message":"With `--macro ashui.ui.Markup.enable()`, templates are Haxe inline markup, and errors inside them point at the exact expression: MarkupWrongType.hx:9, characters 49-63. Completion is weaker. A display request after `count.` inside `\u003ctext\u003e{count.ge}\u003c/text\u003e` returns the top-level list (locals, including count:Signal\u003cInt\u003e; types; literals) instead of Signal's fields (get, set, computed, ptr), which the same expression outside markup gives. It is the same at every offset from -3 to +4 around the cursor.\n\nHow it is parsed: tink_hxx's Parser.parseExpr calls Context.parseInlineString(source, pos) with the embedded expression's own file position. That is the API that should carry the display position into an EDisplay field access. The answer being top-level everywhere suggests the compiler settles the display request at the @:markup literal when the file is parsed, before the macro re-parses the expression; or that parseInlineString's EDisplay is not consulted for a request already answered.\n\nRepro: a class with `var count = Signal.make(2); Owner.root(new LayoutTree(), _ -\u003e \u003cdiv\u003e\u003ctext\u003e{count.ge}\u003c/text\u003e\u003c/div\u003e);`, compiled with the flags in tests/run.sh's haxe_ui and `--display File.hx@\u003coffset after \"count.\"\u003e`. Haxe 4.3.7, tink_hxx 0.25.1.\n\nTo find out: whether Haxe's display for an inline-markup literal can be deferred to a macro's parseInlineString (Haxe's display tests for markup, or vshaxe's), or whether tink_hxx or ashui must map the cursor itself, for example by passing the display position through and rewriting the expression with @:display.","files":null}]}